+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This review covers the production API, its scheduled payment work, persistence, and the scheme, fraud, ledger and notification integrations. It uses the operational context in `okf/` and the implementation as reviewed on 4 November 2026. The service is the customer-facing account view; the core ledger remains the source of truth for money.
+
+Scores are on a 1–10 scale. Severity (S) is 1 = negligible inconvenience and 10 = material financial, regulatory, or data-protection harm. Occurrence (O) is 1 = rare and 10 = frequent/expected. Detection (D) is 1 = almost certain to detect before customer or downstream impact and 10 = unlikely to detect until after impact. RPN = S × O × D. These are inherent/current-control scores, not residual risk after the recommended actions.
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Customer and dependent-system/team effect | Detection / current control | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F1 | Payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService` does not call the available `FraudScreeningClient`, despite policy FRD-7 | Fraudulent payment can leave the account. Fraud team loses its review queue; finance, complaints and operations must investigate an avoidable payment and possible loss | Code review/static rule requiring screening in the payment state machine; metric and alert for payments with no screening decision; reconcile all submitted payments to screening records | 10 | 8 | 8 | **640** | Wire screening before scheme submission. Treat timeout/error as `REFER`/`HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, never send. Persist the decision and correlation ID and test ACCEPT, REFER, REJECT and timeout paths. |
+| F2 | An accepted payment is duplicated | Client retry or concurrent requests creates more than one payment; no idempotency constraint on the customer request/reference and no account-level locking is visible | Customer is paid twice; balance and ledger reconciliation break. Finance investigates settlement differences and support/complaints handle disputes | Daily reconciliation by payment reference, duplicate scheme/ledger references, unusual same-customer repeats; API and DB uniqueness checks are currently absent | 9 | 5 | 8 | **360** | Require an idempotency key, enforce a DB uniqueness constraint, serialize balance/limit checks, and make retries return the existing payment. Preserve the scheme reference `P` + payment ID and ledger reference. |
+| F3 | Scheme timeout is reported as a failed request while the scheme may have accepted it | `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught; the transaction can roll back the local payment after a 10-second ambiguous gateway outcome | Customer may see an error and retry, while the payee receives the original payment. Local history and customer balance view lose the payment; complaints and reconciliation lack a reliable record | Alert on scheme timeouts and compare gateway instruction status/settlement with local payments; current code has no status query or timeout state | 9 | 5 | 9 | **405** | Catch timeout, retain a durable payment with `SUBMITTED`/`UNKNOWN` status, query `/v2/instructions/{reference}`, and reconcile before any retry. Reuse the 30-day scheme reference. |
+| F4 | Ledger posting is missing, duplicated, or unresolved after timeout | Ledger timeout may occur after booking. Operations may retry with a new UUID instead of the original `ledgerReference`; the service exposes `POSTING_FAILED` but no safe retry workflow | Customer view is debited but core money may not be; or customer is debited twice. Finance's 07:00 reconciliation breaks and operations must manually repair it | `POSTING_FAILED` status and daily exceptions report are specified; alert on age/count and reconcile ledger by reference. A retry-reference guard is not implemented | 10 | 4 | 8 | **320** | Make `ledgerReference` deterministic/durable before posting, provide an idempotent repair command using the same reference, query posting status, and block/alert on unresolved exceptions. |
+| F5 | Payment is accepted with an incorrect balance or daily-limit result | The local balance is only a copy; concurrent requests can both pass checks and debit it, and it is overwritten from the ledger at 02:00 | Overspend, declined/late payments, or a customer shown an incorrect balance. Ledger, finance, support and complaints receive inconsistent records | Compare service balances and ledger snapshots; alert on negative/large deltas and daily-limit breaches. The check/debit has no visible locking or atomic conditional update | 9 | 6 | 7 | **378** | Use ledger-authoritative/atomic funds reservation, optimistic locking or per-account serialization, and make balance refresh outcomes observable. Recheck queued payments against current funds and limits before dispatch. |
+| F6 | Queued payment is not sent on its promised working day | 06:00 scheduler does not run, instance is down, job overlaps, or a payment is left `QUEUED`; there is no durable job-run/lease evidence | Customer's bill/rent is late. Fraud review, scheme settlement, support and operations receive stale work; late queued items appear in the exceptions report | Alert for missed 06:00 run, `QUEUED` payments past `executionDate`, and job duration/failure; the past-date query gives a useful recovery set | 8 | 5 | 7 | **280** | Add a durable, idempotent dispatcher/outbox with lease/retry and run audit, expose a replay operation, and page on queued age and failed job runs. |
+| F7 | Payment is submitted on a scheme holiday or wrong date | `WorkingDays` handles weekdays but not bank holidays; scheme rejects with `DT01`. Cut-off and clock configuration can also drift from UK business time | Customer receives a rejection or late payment although the API promised queueing. Scheme, fraud, support and operations handle avoidable failures | Monitor `DT01`, compare execution date with scheme calendar, and test boundary times in Europe/London; current holiday gap is documented | 8 | 5 | 7 | **280** | Use a versioned authoritative UK bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme, calculate next working day from it, and add cut-off/holiday contract tests and calendar-change alerts. |
+| F8 | Customer-facing balance or statement is stale/wrong | Nightly ledger-to-account balance refresh fails, is partial, or runs after readers; statements are cut from the wrong closing balance. The repository contains statement data but no visible generation/refresh process | Customer makes decisions from an incorrect balance; closure, finance reconciliation, regulatory return and complaints records are wrong. A missing/wrong statement can make the return wrong by the 5th working day | Compare every account to the ledger after the 02:00 load; completeness/hash and freshness checks; statement-period completeness report and regulatory pre-submit reconciliation | 9 | 4 | 8 | **288** | Implement a monitored, replayable ledger snapshot import and statement close job with atomic batches, source timestamps, completeness checks, and a hard stop when the regulatory extract is incomplete. |
+| F9 | Account is closed while money remains | Closure approval/finalisation does not perform the required ledger-authoritative zero-balance check; the visible code only records requests and pending approvals | Customer funds can be stranded and operations must remediate by hand. Ledger, support, complaints and regulatory records disagree on account state | Alert on closed accounts with non-zero ledger balance and reconcile closure events; audit approval and finalisation separately | 9 | 3 | 8 | **216** | Implement a single transactional close command that rechecks the ledger balance, outstanding payments and holds immediately before closure; reject non-zero/uncertain results and audit approver, evidence and time. |
+| F10 | Tenant or role boundary exposes data or permits an unsafe action | Incorrect claim mapping, missing method authorization, or a future endpoint bypasses `AccessPolicy`; multi-tenant filtering is not enforced at the database boundary | Cross-tenant disclosure or unauthorized payment/closure. Customers, banks, regulator, security and complaints teams face a high-severity incident | 401/403/404 tests, security regression tests with every role/tenant combination, access-denied metrics and periodic access-log review; current policy intentionally returns 404 for invisible resources | 10 | 2 | 8 | **160** | Add deny-by-default integration tests and tenant predicates/defence-in-depth at repository/service boundaries; require security review for new endpoints and alert on cross-tenant query anomalies. |
+| F11 | Service or database outage loses availability or a committed record | Instance, network, H2-like persistence, or database fails; synchronous calls couple the API request to multiple external systems and there is no visible production backup/restore or outbox design | App and back office cannot read balances, cases or payment status. Requests may be retried, increasing duplicate/ambiguous-payment risk; support, fraud and operations queues age | Health endpoint, latency/error-rate/SLO dashboards, DB backup and restore tests, and synthetic payment/read checks; health currently only exposes a coarse liveness signal | 8 | 4 | 6 | **192** | Use durable production DB with point-in-time recovery, tested restore/failover, readiness checks for required dependencies, request correlation, and an outbox so external work can recover after process failure. |
+| F12 | Notification is absent, misleading, or contains excessive account data | Notification service fails (best effort), a template is wrong, or account-number formatting/template changes leak more than the last four digits | Customer may not know a payment completed or is queued/under review; a full account number/sort code is a reportable data-protection breach. Payment itself must not be undone for notification failure | Notification failure is caught and logged, but there is no retry/dead-letter path or content scan; synthetic template tests and DLP alerting are needed | 7 | 3 | 7 | **147** | Publish notification events through a durable outbox with retry/DLQ and status visibility, keep payment non-blocking, and add contract tests/DLP assertions that only `accountEnding` is emitted. |
+| F13 | Operational exception is not detected or acted on within its window | Logging is mostly WARN, metrics/runbooks are not part of the service, or `POSTING_FAILED`, held-for-review and stale-queue reports are not delivered | Fraud review can miss its four-hour target; posting failures remain unresolved; customers and dependent teams discover problems through complaints or reconciliation | The required reports are described in the context, but delivery/ownership/SLA evidence is not present in code | 8 | 6 | 9 | **432** | Define owners and SLAs for fraud holds, posting failures and overdue queues; emit structured events/metrics, page on age thresholds, and run daily reconciliation with acknowledged exceptions. |
+
+## Priority and release gates
+
+The highest-priority release blockers are F1 (unscreened payments), F3 (ambiguous scheme timeout), F13 (undetected operational exceptions), F5 (concurrent/stale balance controls), F8 (ledger snapshot/statements), and F4 (safe ledger retry). A release should also gate on:
+
+- no payment can reach the scheme without a persisted fraud decision;
+- scheme and ledger timeout tests prove that retries preserve the original reference and do not create a second payment;
+- queued, held, and posting-failed items have durable ownership, age-based alerts, and replay/reconciliation procedures;
+- balance refresh and statement completeness are reconciled against the ledger; and
+- tenant isolation, closure zero-balance checks, notification redaction, and backup restore are covered by automated tests or operational evidence.
+
+The RPN is a prioritisation aid. Any severity-10 financial, fraud, regulatory, or data-protection failure remains a release concern even when its RPN is lower because occurrence is currently estimated as low.
